tmp/phase-17-runs/S-13.log:633:docs/playwright-interop.md:184:- **Expression inspection:** when the adapter supports evaluation, pause in the page script and use `npx dap-cli evaluate` for DOM or application state reads before resuming.
tmp/phase-17-runs/S-13.log:634:docs/playwright-interop.md:185:- **Reference refresh:** treat `threadId`, `frameId`, and `variablesReference` values as single-pause handles. Reacquire them after every `continue`, step, restart, or new stopped event.
tmp/phase-17-runs/S-13.log:635:docs/playwright-interop.md:201:- **No `stopped` event after the click.** Inspect `npx dap-cli threads`. An empty thread list after Chrome attach means js-debug did not select a browser page target. If threads exist, inspect the `breakpoints set` response; an unbound breakpoint means the source path, URL mapping, or `webRoot` shape still does not match the browser-loaded script.
tmp/phase-17-runs/S-13.log:636:docs/playwright-interop.md:202:- **`playwright-cli attach` fails with `connection refused`.** The launch in Terminal 1 did not include `--remote-debugging-port=9222` in `runtimeArgs`, or Chromium is still starting up. Wait a second and retry.
tmp/phase-17-runs/S-13.log:637:docs/playwright-interop.md:203:- **`playwright-cli click` hangs.** That is correct behavior — the page is paused at your breakpoint. Either resume from Terminal 1 (`npx dap-cli continue --thread-id 0`) or use `playwright-cli eval` instead of `click` to dispatch the DOM event without auto-wait.
